Perfect — minimal and visible. I'll give one tiny standalone directive plus the two smallest edits you need (one import in the component and one attribute in the template). That’s it — one small change proves you implemented a custom directive and has zero risk of breaking other code.

I recommend appAutofocus — it’s extremely small, useful in sign-in, and requires only:

1. add autofocus.directive.ts, and


2. add the directive to SignInComponent imports + add appAutofocus attribute to the phone <input>.




---

1) Create this file

Path: src/app/shared/directives/autofocus.directive.ts
Contents: (copy-paste)

import { Directive, ElementRef, AfterViewInit } from '@angular/core';

@Directive({
  selector: '[appAutofocus]',
  standalone: true
})
export class AutofocusDirective implements AfterViewInit {
  constructor(private el: ElementRef<HTMLInputElement | HTMLTextAreaElement>) {}

  ngAfterViewInit(): void {
    // small timeout so it works reliably in many render/animation scenarios
    setTimeout(() => {
      try { this.el.nativeElement.focus(); }
      catch { /* ignore if element not focusable */ }
    }, 20);
  }
}


---

2) Add the directive to the Sign-in component imports

Open your sign-in component TypeScript file (example path: src/app/features/auth/sign-in/sign-in.component.ts) and add the import and include it in the component imports array.

Find the top of the file and add:

import { AutofocusDirective } from '../../../shared/directives/autofocus.directive';

(Adjust relative path if your folder structure differs.)

Then in the @Component({ ... }) metadata, update imports: [...] to include AutofocusDirective. Example snippet:

@Component({
  selector: 'app-sign-in',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [CommonModule, ReactiveFormsModule, RouterModule, AutofocusDirective],
  templateUrl: './sign-in.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./sign-in.component.scss']
})
export class SignInComponent { ... }

> If your component uses inline template: \...`, just ensure you still add AutofocusDirectivetoimports`.




---

3) Add the attribute to the phone input in the template

Open sign-in.component.html (or inline template) and locate the phone input. Change:

<input formControlName="phone" inputmode="tel" />

to:

<input formControlName="phone" inputmode="tel" appAutofocus />

That’s it — one attribute added.


---

4) How to test (one command)

Run (or restart) the app and verify autofocus:

ng serve

Open the app (or refresh). On the Sign In page the phone input will be focused automatically. That visible behavior demonstrates the directive working.


